r/id #uuid "bce4f31e-2daa-5469-ae29-c4736cf33aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-1)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bce4f31e-2daa-5469-ae29-c4736cf33aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-1)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245999} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5d1fe0-66e0-5072-aedc-5e9a7b18459a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246822}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "904dff05-ecb6-51b3-917d-26bcf8537c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-112615" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6.$ We know that $(x-3)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $-13$ | $7$ | $17$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $-12$ | $-15$ | $6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6}{x-3}=3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-7$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2}{x+1}=3{x}^{2}-7x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1)(3{x}^{2}-7x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1){{(3x-1)(x-2)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3x-1)(x-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b24578e-481e-553a-a83a-dc64b6635d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244416} {:answer/id #uuid "39f82396-0c2d-56e1-b9fd-de4a09dd64c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244827} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcb12f3-3780-53db-adc2-7c2e38923858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-3)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245004} {:answer/id #uuid "b902a4ce-fd1e-5700-be25-6073ff3196a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x+2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245966} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb9253d-1f88-5f8c-9b58-b795ed407dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4x-3)(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f82396-0c2d-56e1-b9fd-de4a09dd64c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-2)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244827} :answer-field/id #uuid "14ae9a65-2c31-50dc-9b0c-6b6e3de50ea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246584}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4055097-e27e-5747-8711-3ff850d11a98" :question/math-academy-id "q-112617" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24.$ We know that $(x-3)$ and $(x+4)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $4$ | $-37$ | $-14$ | $24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $39$ | $6$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $3$ | $13$ | $2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24}{x-3}=3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+4)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $3$ | $13$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-12$ | $-4$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8}{x+4}=3{x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4)(3{x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4){{(3x-2)(x+1)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3x-2)(x+1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "292959fc-5935-574c-9f04-13e8671cf522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-7)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244674} {:answer/id #uuid "36ddaff0-33e2-5585-b7bd-e8cc98dc6e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244800} {:answer/id #uuid "45503d60-7034-5dc8-94fc-0693fe24418a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(5-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244928} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbc7b91-18d2-5c35-b07b-b9f5fa63305c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2-x)(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246129} {:answer/id #uuid "f620a82c-3ecf-57d2-9154-b09ad9b1016b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)(x+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36ddaff0-33e2-5585-b7bd-e8cc98dc6e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244800} :answer-field/id #uuid "31c5333b-8831-529d-9a73-a9a967c8862b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246629}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c8eab9f-7b10-5db7-a80e-51f13f149526" :question/math-academy-id "q-112626" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35.$ We know that $(x-5)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-5)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division:\n\n\\\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-36$ | $-2$ | $35$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $5$ | $35$ | $-5$ | $-35$ |\n|  | $1$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35}{x-5}={x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $-7$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $7$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-7$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7}{x+1}={x}^{2}+6x-7,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1)({x}^{2}+6x-7).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1){{(x-1)(x+7)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x-1)(x+7).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18490f60-4edc-508c-abe1-e7e0f13c065d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285003} {:answer/id #uuid "312ee193-d577-5512-a0e8-1cc54605d5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285231} {:answer/id #uuid "e405d079-ad9d-55f2-b5eb-d4d63e4f65f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286871} {:answer/id #uuid "eb434cbb-a7e4-590f-89bc-63a9f59cf077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286942} {:answer/id #uuid "f66903c6-f94c-504b-bfea-93036d198d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-x)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "312ee193-d577-5512-a0e8-1cc54605d5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285231} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ecf3453-145b-5f3a-bf2b-36b631e0d173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287351}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a78f6b62-d8f4-5963-b2aa-dcc684d0394b" :question/math-academy-id "q-18862" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3)P(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-11$ | $9$ | $18$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $6$ | $-15$ | $-18$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18}{x-3}={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-3)({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-2)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $8$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6}{x-2}={x}^{2}+4x+3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3)({x}^{2}+4x+3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3){{(x+1)(x+3)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x+1)(x+3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304777 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc6878dd-7880-56e1-b90f-bffe1457db87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc6878dd-7880-56e1-b90f-bffe1457db87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304039} :answer-field/id #uuid "65363172-90a7-5c3c-8285-eb6ba0e5a62c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304349}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c8e3cf5-7e90-5470-a693-ef40ffd528ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-328505" :question/problem "The polynomial \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12\n$$\n\n \ncan be expressed as\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-3)\\cdot P(x).\n$$\n\nFind the polynomial $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $16$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-10$ | $12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12}{x-2}={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $3$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6}{x-3}={x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)(x-3)({x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(x)={x}^{2}+x-2=(x+2)(x-1).\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial Given Two Factors"}]}
